<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hintsa kaKhawuta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1790–<a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hintsa kaKhawuta was king of the Gcaleka Xhosa, paramount among the Xhosa chiefs, from about 1820 to 1835. During the Sixth Frontier War the British accused him of harbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, and he came to the camp of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-durban">Benjamin D'Urban</a> to negotiate. Held as a hostage, he was shot dead while trying to escape in May 1835, and his body was mutilated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hintsa-kakhawuta.jpg" alt="Hintsa kaKhawuta" width="170" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anon. Cape Colony. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chief_Hintsa_of_the_Gcaleka_Xhosa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
